  {
    "question_text": "Simplify $\\sqrt{(x-8)^2}$ when (a) $x\\ge8$ and (b) $x<8$.",
    "solution_text": "(a) Since $x-8\\ge0$, its absolute value is $x-8$.\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sqrt{(x-8)^2}\\\\&=|x-8|\\\\&=x-8\\end{align*}$\n(b) Since $x-8<0$, negate the expression inside the absolute value.\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sqrt{(x-8)^2}\\\\&=|x-8|\\\\&=-(x-8)\\\\&=8-x\\end{align*}$"
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Simplify $\\sqrt{x^2-8x+16}+\\sqrt{x^2+6x+9}$ for $-3\\le x\\le4$.",
    "solution_text": "On this interval, $x-4\\le0$ and $x+3\\ge0$, so $|x-4|=4-x$ and $|x+3|=x+3$.\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sqrt{x^2-8x+16}+\\sqrt{x^2+6x+9}\\\\&=\\sqrt{(x-4)^2}+\\sqrt{(x+3)^2}\\\\&=|x-4|+|x+3|\\\\&=(4-x)+(x+3)\\\\&=7\\end{align*}$"
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Simplify (a) $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{(x-9)^2}}{x-9}$ and (b) $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{(x-9)^2}}{9-x}$, given $x<9$.",
    "solution_text": "Since $x<9$, $|x-9|=-(x-9)=9-x$. Both denominators are non-zero.\n(a)\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\frac{\\sqrt{(x-9)^2}}{x-9}\\\\&=\\frac{|x-9|}{x-9}\\\\&=\\frac{-(x-9)}{x-9}\\\\&=-1\\end{align*}$\n(b)\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\frac{\\sqrt{(x-9)^2}}{9-x}\\\\&=\\frac{|x-9|}{9-x}\\\\&=\\frac{9-x}{9-x}\\\\&=1\\end{align*}$"
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Simplify $\\sqrt{x^4-8x^2+16}$ for $|x|<2$.",
    "solution_text": "Since $|x|<2$, $x^2<4$ and hence $x^2-4<0$.\n$\\begin{align*}&\\mathrel{\\phantom{=}}\\sqrt{x^4-8x^2+16}\\\\&=\\sqrt{(x^2-4)^2}\\\\&=|x^2-4|\\\\&=-(x^2-4)\\\\&=4-x^2\\end{align*}$"
  }
]
